Primary Sjögren's Syndrome
Complex Fatigue Entities
Autoimmune exocrinopathy with systemic features

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-040
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe dysphagia, acute ocular pain, and profound systemic exhaustion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, dramatic worsening of severe dry mouth preventing the swallowing of any solid food, intense bilateral eye burning, and immobilizing fatigue. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to rule out acute corneal abrasion/ulceration or esophageal obstruction, and to provide potent systemic relief before standard clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My eyes feel like they are full of broken glass, my mouth is so dry I cannot swallow, and my whole body aches.
History of Present Illness: Patient with known Primary Sjögren's Syndrome reports a severe acute exacerbation over the last 48 hours. Symptoms include extreme sicca: intense burning and foreign-body sensation in both eyes, and absolute xerostomia preventing speech without sipping water. Additionally, the patient reports profound fatigue and diffuse bilateral arthralgia affecting the hands and knees. The patient has been unable to eat solid food today due to the lack of saliva.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to systemic exhaustion and severe ocular discomfort in ambient light.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of inadequate caloric intake and dental/corneal damage during this severe flare.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 66 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, keeping eyes closed, sipping water constantly to allow speech.
Physical Exam: Ocular exam reveals severe bilateral conjunctival injection and lack of tear pooling; no focal corneal opacities or purulent discharge. Oral cavity is parched; tongue is red, lobulated, with absence of salivary pooling under the tongue. Parotid glands are mildly enlarged bilaterally but non-tender and without erythema. Mild, symmetrical, boggy swelling noted in the bilateral MCP joints. Presentation confirms a severe flare of autoimmune exocrinopathy with systemic features.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty water bottles and over-the-counter eye drop containers scattered by the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Using standard artificial tears every 30 minutes with inadequate relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Primary Sjögren's Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Corneal Ulcer/Keratitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetrical presentation rather than a unilateral, exquisitely painful focal defect, and absence of purulent discharge.

Acute Parotitis (Bacterial) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, non-tender parotid enlargement without severe localized pain, high fever, or purulent exudate from Stensen's duct.

Fibromyalgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of stark, objective autoimmune exocrinopathy (severe objective dry eye/mouth and parotid swelling) rather than purely subjective pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The acute, severe exacerbation of ocular and oral sicca symptoms posed an immediate risk to the integrity of the cornea and the ability to maintain oral nutrition. An urgent home assessment was required to definitively exclude sight-threatening acute corneal ulceration and bacterial parotitis. Having confirmed a severe systemic autoimmune flare, immediate intervention with intensive topical lubrication and systemic anti-inflammatories was required to break the pain cycle, preserve ocular surface integrity, and manage the profound systemic fatigue before regular hours.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-viscosity, preservative-free lubricating eye ointment (e.g., Poly Visc) for immediate overnight use, and a short course of Meloxicam 15mg daily for the systemic arthralgia. Targeted intervention strategy includes Low Dose Tadalafil (microvascular), LDN, and Omega-3. Advised on the use of saliva substitutes.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying the thick, preservative-free eye ointment exactly 10 minutes before sleep, you will physically seal moisture onto your cornea, directly preventing the abrasive damage of your eyelids opening and rewarding yourself with pain-free, comfortable eyes upon waking.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Room humidifier strongly recommended.
Follow-up: Urgent GP and Optometrist review within 3 days for formal slit-lamp examination and review of systemic immunomodulators.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if vision suddenly drops or becomes highly blurred, if a single eye becomes intensely painful and light-sensitive, or if sudden high fever and localized jaw/cheek swelling occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Primary Sjögren's involves both the exocrine glands and systemic inflammation; severe ocular flares risk corneal damage and require expert optometric monitoring.
Clinical Course: The intensive overnight lubrication and oral anti-inflammatories will significantly reduce the burning and joint pain within 24 hours. The profound fatigue will improve more gradually.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the Emergency Department or an emergency eye hospital immediately if you suddenly lose vision, or if one cheek becomes fiery red, hot, and extremely painful.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is actively attacking your moisture-producing glands; the thick eye ointment acts as a physical shield for your eyes, while the oral medication rapidly turns down the systemic inflammation causing your joint pain.
Actionable Steps: Exclusively eat soft, blended, or soup-based meals for the next 48 hours; removing the need for intense chewing and saliva production rests your salivary glands, positively reinforcing a faster recovery of your mouth tissues.

